.TH "SQL-LINT" "1" "Jun 03, 2025" "" "sql-lint"
.SH NAME
sql-lint \- sql-lint Documentation
.SH INTRODUCTION
.SS What is sql\-lint?
.sp
\fBsql\-lint\fP is a linter for SQL dialects. It currently supports MySQL and
Postgres. It brings errors to your attention, suggests what\(aqs wrong with them,
why it may be wrong, and what you can do as a developer to fix it. Generally
these errors are more verbose and specific than those coming from an SQL server.
.sp
Here\(aqs a small excerpt of its use:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
: sql\-lint test/test\-files//test.sql 
test/test\-files//test.sql:16 [sql\-lint: unmatched\-parentheses] Unmatched parentheses.
test/test\-files//test.sql:20 [sql\-lint: missing\-where] DELETE statement missing WHERE clause.
test/test\-files//test.sql:22 [sql\-lint: invalid\-drop\-option] Option \(aqthing\(aq is not a valid option, must be one of \(aq[\(dqdatabase\(dq,\(dqevent\(dq,\(dqfunction\(dq,\(dqindex\(dq,\(dqlogfile\(dq,\(dqprocedure\(dq,\(dqschema\(dq,\(dqserver\(dq,\(dqtable\(dq,\(dqview\(dq,\(dqtablespace\(dq,\(dqtrigger\(dq]\(aq.
test/test\-files//test.sql:26 [sql\-lint: invalid\-truncate\-option] Option \(aqsomething\(aq is not a valid option, must be one of \(aq[\(dqtable\(dq]\(aq.
test/test\-files//test.sql:30 [sql\-lint: odd\-code\-point] Unexpected code point.
test/test\-files//test.sql:32 [sql\-lint: invalid\-limit\-quantifier] Argument \(aqtest\(aq is not a valid quantifier for LIMIT clause.
test/test\-files//test.sql:24 [ER_PARSE_ERROR] You have an error in your SQL syntax; check the manual that corresponds to your MySQL server version for the right syntax to use near \(aqCREATE test person\(aq at line 1
test/test\-files//test.sql:39 [ER_NO_SUCH_TABLE] Table \(aqsymfony.dont_exist\(aq doesn\(aqt exist
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Usage
.sp
\fBsql\-lint\fP is used from the command line in several ways.
.SS Via stdin
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
echo \(dqDELETE FROM person;\(dq | sql\-lint
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS With a file
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
sql\-lint test\-file.sql
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Command line options
.SS \-V \-\-version
.sp
The version of \fBsql\-lint\fP\&.
Useful for bug reports and confirming what features are available to you.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
sql\-lint \-\-version
> 0.0.11
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \-d \-\-driver
.sp
\fBmysql\fP | \fBpostgres\fP
.sp
Default is \fBmysql\fP\&.
.sp
The driver to use.
.SS \-v \-\-verbose
.sp
How verbose to be with output. \fB\-v\fP will print out the output from the lexer.
Usually you do not want any verbosity. Useful for bug reports and debugging.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
sql\-lint \-\-verbose
> ...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \-\-config
.sp
The path for the configuration file.
.sp
Default is \fB$HOME/.config/sql\-lint/config.json\fP
.SS \-\-format
.sp
\fBsimple\fP | \fBjson\fP
.sp
Default is \fBsimple\fP\&.
.sp
The output format of \fBsql\-lint\fP\&.
.sp
\fBsimple\fP is the most user friendly and human readable. You won\(aqt usually change
the format unless you have a reason to.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
echo \(aqDELETE FROM person;\(aq | sql\-lint
> stdin:1 [sql\-lint: missing\-where] DELETE statement missing WHERE clause.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBjson\fP can be used if you wish. Usually this is done for editor
integration or for consumption via some other service.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
echo \(aqDELETE FROM person;\(aq | sql\-lint \-\-format json
> {
     \(dqsource\(dq:\(dqstdin\(dq,
     \(dqerror\(dq:\(dq[sql\-lint: missing\-where] DELETE statement missing WHERE clause.\(dq,
     \(dqline\(dq:1
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \-\-host
.sp
The host for the connection.
.SS \-\-user
.sp
The user for the connection.
.SS \-\-password
.sp
The password for the connection.
.SS \-\-database
.sp
Optional, default is no database, where you will have to specify the database in your queries.
.sp
The database for the connection.
.SS \-\-port
.sp
Default is \fB3306\fP\&.
.sp
The port for the connection.
.SS \-h \-\-help
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
: sql\-lint \-h
Usage: sql\-lint [options]

Lint sql files and stdin for errors, oddities, and bad practices.

Options:
  \-V, \-\-version                output the version number
  \-\-fix [string]               The .sql string to fix (experimental and alpha)
  \-d, \-\-driver <string>        The driver to use, must be one of [\(aqmysql\(aq, \(aqpostgres\(aq]
  \-v, \-\-verbose                Brings back information on the what it\(aqs linting and the tokens generated
  \-\-format <string>            The format of the output, can be one of [\(aqsimple\(aq, \(aqjson\(aq] (default: \(dqsimple\(dq)
  \-\-host <string>              The host for the database connection
  \-\-user <string>              The user for the database connection
  \-\-password <string>          The password for the database connection
  \-\-database <string>          The database for the database connection
  \-\-port <string>              The port for the database connection
  \-\-config <string>            The path to the configuration file
  \-\-ignore\-errors <string...>  The errors to ignore (comma separated)
  \-h, \-\-help                   display help for command
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Programmatic Access
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
import sqlLint from \(aqsql\-lint\(aq

// using async/await

const errors = await sqlLint({
  sql: \(aqSELECT my_column FROM my_table\(aq,
})

// or using promise

sqlLint({ sql: \(aqSELECT my_column FROM my_table\(aq }).then(errors => {
  for (const error of errors) {
    // do something
  }
})

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Parameters
.sp
sql\-lint accepts an object using the following interface as its only argument
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  sql: string
  host?: string
  user?: string
  port?: number
  driver?: string
  prefix?: string
  password?: string
  verbosity?: number
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Notes on some of the parameters
.sp
\fBsql\fP: can have multiple queries separated by \fB;\fP
.sp
\fBhost\fP: if host is not provided \fBsql\-lint\fP will only perform checks that do not require a connection
.sp
\fBdriver\fP: defaults to mysql
.sp
\fBport\fP: if port is not provided it will use the default port for the driver you are using
.SS Output
.sp
sql\-lint returns an \fBarray\fP of objects with the following shape
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  line: number
  error: string
  source: string
  additionalInformation: string
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH INSTALLATION
.sp
Installation is simple. Download a pre\-built
\X'tty: link https://github.com/joereynolds/sql-lint/releases'\fI\%binary\fP\X'tty: link' from Github. Once
you\(aqve installed \fBsql\-lint\fP, you will want to \fI\%configure it\fP for the best
experience.
.sp
\fBsql\-lint\fP supports Mac, Linux, and Windows.
.sp
If you prefer, you can do \fBnpm i \-g sql\-lint\fP or \fByarn global add sql\-lint\fP if you\(aqre using yarn.
.SS Programmatic Access
.sp
For programmatic access you\(aqll want to instead install \fBsql\-lint\fP into your node project with \fBnpm i sql\-lint\fP or \fByarn add sql\-lint\fP\&.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.sp
Configuring \fBsql\-lint\fP to connect to your database of choice allows even more
errors to come through. Errors that \fBsql\-lint\fP wouldn\(aqt find itself. To do this
is easy, simply supply the connection details to your database in one of two
ways:
.SS Via CLI
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
sql\-lint \-\-driver=\(dqmysql\(dq \-\-host=\(dqlocalhost\(dq \-\-user=\(dqroot\(dq \-\-password=\(dqhunter2\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Via File
.sp
\fBsql\-lint\fP will search the current working directory and its parent directories
for a configuration file \fB\&.sql\-lint.json\fP\&. This allows you to have
directory\-local configurations for different projects. If no \fB\&.sql\-lint.json\fP
is found, it will fall back to the global configuration file.
.sp
A global configuration file for \fBsql\-lint\fP can reside in
\fB~/.config/sql\-lint/config.json\fP\&.  It follows the \X'tty: link https://specifications.freedesktop.org/basedir-spec/basedir-spec-latest.html'\fI\%XDG Base Directory
Specification\fP\X'tty: link'\&.
Specifically, it uses \fB$HOME/.config\fP\&.
.sp
You can also manually specify a path for the config with the \fB\-\-config\fP flag.
.sp
You should put the following in there for more intelligent errors to come through
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqdriver\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq,
    \(dqhost\(dq: \(dqlocalhost\(dq,
    \(dquser\(dq: \(dqroot\(dq,
    \(dqpassword\(dq: \(dqhunter2\(dq,
    \(dqport\(dq: 3306,
    \(dqdatabase\(dq: \(dqyour_database\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Configuration options
.sp
An exhaustive list of the configuration options for your \fBconfig.json\fP file are
below.
.SS \fBdriver\fP
.sp
The driver to be used to check for errors.
Accepted ones are \fBmysql\fP and \fBpostgres\fP\&.
.sp
Optional, default is \fBmysql\fP\&.
.SS \fBhost\fP
.sp
The host of the database server.
.SS \fBuser\fP
.sp
The user for the database server.
.SS \fBpassword\fP
.sp
The password for the database server.
.SS \fBport\fP
.sp
The port to connect to.
.sp
Optional, default is \fB3306\fP\&.
.SS \fBdatabase\fP
.sp
The database to connect to.
.sp
Optional, default is no database, where you will have to specify the database in your queries.
.SS \fBignore\-errors\fP
.sp
Don\(aqt want to be warned about a particular error?
In that case add it to the \fBignore\-errors\fP array in \fB~/.config/sql\-lint/config.json\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqhost\(dq: \(dqlocalhost\(dq,
    \(dquser\(dq: \(dqroot\(dq,
    \(dqpassword\(dq: \(dqpassword\(dq,
    \(dqignore\-errors\(dq: [
        \(dqodd\-code\-point\(dq,
        \(dqmissing\-where\(dq
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The example above will skip checks for odd code points and \fBDELETE\fP statements with missing \fBWHERE\fP clauses.
.sp
For a full list of all available checks, see \fI\%the check
documentation\fP
.sp
You cannot skip checks that are returned from the DB server itself, only the checks built into \fBsql\-lint\fP\&.
.sp
Note that this option is also available as a flag on the cli.
i.e.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
sql\-lint \-\-ignore\-errors=trailing\-whitespace some\-sql\-file.sql
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Multiple errors can be comma separated:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
sql\-lint \-\-ignore\-errors=trailing\-whitespace,missing\-where,hungarian\-notation some\-sql\-file.sql
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Example configuration
.sp
The below configuration contains every option available.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqhost\(dq: \(dqlocalhost\(dq,
    \(dquser\(dq: \(dqroot\(dq,
    \(dqpassword\(dq: \(dqpassword\(dq,
    \(dqport\(dq: 3306,
    \(dqdatabase\(dq: \(dqyour_database\(dq,
    \(dqignore\-errors\(dq: [
        \(dqodd\-code\-point\(dq,
        \(dqmissing\-where\(dq,
        \(dqinvalid\-drop\-option\(dq,
        \(dqinvalid\-create\-option\(dq,
        \(dqinvalid\-truncate\-option\(dq,
        \(dqinvalid\-alter\-option\(dq,
        \(dqhungarian\-notation\(dq,
        \(dqtrailing\-whitespace\(dq
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS A word of warning
.sp
Do not version control your configuration file unless you know what you\(aqre
doing. Stick it in your global \fB\&.gitignore\fP to be safe.
.SS Editor Integration
.sp
\fBsql\-lint\fP can integrate with any editor that supports external plugins.
.SS Vim / Neovim
.SS Ale
.sp
\fBsql\-lint\fP can be integrated into (Neo)Vim with \X'tty: link https://github.com/dense-analysis/ale/'\fI\%Ale\fP\X'tty: link'\&.
.SS Vanilla
.sp
If you want to go without a plugin, the simplest option is to run the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
:!sql\-lint %
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH CHECKS
.sp
\fBsql\-lint\fP comes with its own suite of checks. Aside from its own checks, it
also returns any errors from the SQL server you have connected to. Generally
you\(aqll find that the errors from \fBsql\-lint\fP are more informative than those from
the server. That said, you will still want errors from the server as it covers
more cases and will catch things that \fBsql\-lint\fP does not.
.SS Reference
.SS unmatched\-parentheses
.sp
Shown when a query has an unbalanced amount of parentheses.
.SS Example output
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
test/test\-files//test.sql:16 [sql\-lint: unmatched\-parentheses] Unmatched parentheses.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS missing\-where
.sp
Shown when a \fBDELETE\fP statement is missing a \fBWHERE\fP clause.
.SS Example output
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
test/test\-files/test.sql:20 [sql\-lint: missing\-where] DELETE statement missing WHERE clause.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS invalid\-drop\-option
.sp
Shown when an invalid option is given to the \fBDROP\fP statement.
.SS Example output
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
test/test\-files/test.sql:22 [sql\-lint: invalid\-drop\-option] Option \(aqthing\(aq is not a valid option, must be one of \(aq[\(dqdatabase\(dq,\(dqevent\(dq,\(dqfunction\(dq,\(dqindex\(dq,\(dqlogfile\(dq,\(dqprocedure\(dq,\(dqschema\(dq,\(dqserver\(dq,\(dqtable\(dq,\(dqview\(dq,\(dqtablespace\(dq,\(dqtrigger\(dq]\(aq.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS invalid\-create\-option
.sp
Shown when an invalid option is given to the \fBCREATE\fP statement.
.SS Example output
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
:24 [sql\-lint: invalid\-create\-option] Option \(aqtest\(aq is not a valid option, must be one of \(aq[\(dqalgorithm\(dq,\(dqdatabase\(dq,\(dqdefiner\(dq,\(dqevent\(dq,\(dqfunction\(dq,\(dqindex\(dq,\(dqor\(dq,\(dqprocedure\(dq,\(dqserver\(dq,\(dqtable\(dq,\(dqtablespace\(dq,\(dqtemporary\(dq,\(dqtrigger\(dq,\(dquser\(dq,\(dqunique\(dq,\(dqview\(dq]\(aq.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS invalid\-truncate\-option
.sp
Shown when an invalid option is given to the \fBTRUNCATE\fP statement.
.SS Example output
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
test/test\-files/test.sql:26 [sql\-lint: invalid\-truncate\-option] Option \(aqsomething\(aq is not a valid option, must be one of \(aq[\(dqtable\(dq]\(aq.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS invalid\-alter\-option
.sp
Shown when an invalid option is given to the \fBALTER\fP statement.
.SS Example output
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
test/test\-files/test.sql:28 [sql\-lint: invalid\-alter\-option] Option \(aqmlady\(aq is not a valid option, must be one of \(aq[\(dqcolumn\(dq,\(dqonline\(dq,\(dqoffline\(dq,\(dqignore\(dq,\(dqdatabase\(dq,\(dqevent\(dq,\(dqfunction\(dq,\(dqprocedure\(dq,\(dqserver\(dq,\(dqtable\(dq,\(dqtablespace\(dq,\(dqview\(dq]\(aq.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS odd\-code\-point
.sp
Shown when there are unsupported/unusual* code points in your code.
.sp
*This check came about whilst working Microsoft Excel. Microsoft likes to
add a lot of zany characters which can subtly break your data without you
realising.
.SS Example output
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
test/test\-files//test.sql:30 [sql\-lint: odd\-code\-point] Unexpected code point.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS invalid\-limit\-quantifier
.sp
Shown when you specify something other than a number to the \fBLIMIT\fP statement.
.SS Example output
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
test/test\-files//test.sql:32 [sql\-lint: invalid\-limit\-quantifier] Argument \(aqtest\(aq is not a valid quantifier for LIMIT clause.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS hungarian\-notation
.sp
Shown when the string \fBsp_\fP or \fBtbl_\fP is present in the query.
.SS Example output
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
test/test\-files/test.sql:34 [sql\-lint: hungarian\-notation] Hungarian notation present in query
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS trailing\-whitespace
.sp
Shown when a query has trailing whitespace.
.SS Example output
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
test/test\-files/test.sql:34 [sql\-lint: trailing\-whitespace] Trailing whitespace
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.SS I\(aqm not seeing any warnings
.sp
Run \fBsql\-lint your\-file\fP and it will display the exception.
Add the \fB\-v\fP flag for more information.
.SS It\(aqs telling me there\(aqs a syntax error when there\(aqs clearly not.
.sp
Chances are you\(aqre using an old(er) version of MySQL.
\fBEXPLAIN\fPing on \fBINSERT|UPDATE|DELETE\fP was added in Mysql 5.6.
.SH DEVELOPMENT
.sp
If you\(aqre interested in helping further the development of \fBsql\-lint\fP then read
on. Casual users can ignore this section.
.SS How it works
.sp
A raw query (either from stdin, a file, or a string) hits \fBmain.ts\fP\&.
This query then gets categorised into the type of statement it is (\fBSELECT\fP,
\fBINSERT\fP, \fBUPDATE\fP, \fBDELETE\fP etc...), as the SQL grammar is pretty damn huge, there is
a lexer per statement. This adds redundancy but increases flexibility.
.sp
Once a query has been categorised, it is then lexxed by the relevant lexer. See the
\fBsrc/lexer\fP directory for the inner workings.
.sp
i.e. if we have the statement
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
SELECT name FROM user
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This will hit the lexer which will categorise this as a \fBSELECT\fP statement which
the \fBSELECT\fP lexer will then tokenise. The tokenised string is then
passed through to every checker to look for any linting errors.
.SS Adding a check
.sp
If you want to add your own check, read on. It\(aqs quite simple but also verbose.
.sp
This can probably be automated to make it WAY easier.
.sp
Anyway, here are the steps.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Create a check under \fBsrc/checker/checks\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
The name of the class is also the name of the checker so name it well
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
Add your check to \fBsrc/barrel/checks.ts\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
All checks live here so we can import them all conveniently
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
Import your check in \fBsrc/checker/checkFactory.ts\fP
.IP \(bu 2
Add your check to the \fBcheckMap\fP in \fBsrc/checker/checkFactory.ts\fP
.IP \(bu 2
Add it to the README.md so people know it\(aqs a thing
.IP \(bu 2
Add it to \fBconfiguration.md\fP\&. This is an exhaustive list of the checks
.IP \(bu 2
Add tests. The name of the test should match the name of the check
.IP \(bu 2
Add it to \fBchecks.md\fP, the main documentation for checks
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnpm run build\fP to compile the changes
.UNINDENT
.SS Troubleshooting
.SS \fBTypeError: checkMap[check] is not a constructor\fP
.sp
Your check is not being picked up by the \fBcheckerRunner\fP\&. log out what the value
of \fBchecks\fP is in \fBcheckerRunner\fP \fBafter\fP the \fBsplice\fPing.
.SS Testing the code
.sp
Testing requires sql\-lint to be installed.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
npm install \-g sql\-lint
\&./build/build.sh //This will run more than just the tests (recommended)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Using the Docker container
.sp
First, make sure port \fB3306\fP is available locally.  (You can do this by
inspecting the output of \fBsudo lsof \-i :3306\fP and \fBdocker ps\fP and \fBkill\fPing
anything using that port) Now do:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
docker\-compose up \-\-build \-d \-\-force\-recreate
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
At this point the container(s) will be up and ready to use.  You can login with
the following credentials: \fBmysql \-u root \-ppassword\fP\&.
.sp
Here\(aqs an example of a query:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
docker exec sqllint_mysql_1 mysql \-u root \-ppassword \-e \(dqSHOW DATABASES\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Connecting sql\-lint to the Docker container
.sp
Change your config file in \fB~/.config/sql\-lint/config.json\fP to have the following values:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqdriver\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq,
    \(dqhost\(dq: \(dqlocalhost\(dq,
    \(dquser\(dq: \(dqroot\(dq,
    \(dqpassword\(dq: \(dqpassword\(dq,
    \(dqport\(dq: 3306
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Optionally, you can also add \fB\(dqdatabase\(dq: \(dqyour_database_name\(dq\fP if you want to connect
to a specific database. However, most tests were written without this option.
.SS This documentation
.sp
This documentation is built on \fBsphinx\fP and \fBreadthedocs\fP\&. To run it locally,
you will need the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBsql\-lint\fP repository (documentation lies in \fBdocs/\fP)
.IP \(bu 2
